Patches next 16.4.0's "use cache" wrapper, both its CommonJS and ESM copies.

Defect: concurrent requests for the same cache key share one in-progress fill, and a request that
joins another's fill takes its result without checking it against the tags that expired after the
fill started. The leader path runs those checks only on a cache handler's entry and on reuse within
one request. So a recipe list read that starts just after a delete's updateTag can join a fill that
began before the delete and show the deleted recipe for one response; create and edit, which call
updateTag and then redirect, share the window. Upstream canary has the same code.

Fix: before using a joined fill, run the leader path's checks in its order (refresh the handler's
tags, read the implicit tags' expiration, shouldDiscardCacheEntry), then ask the kind's handler for
each of the fill's tags' expiration. An expiration after the fill started and not in the future
sends the request round the loop to regenerate, at the cost of one extra fill for each joiner that
races a tag expiration.

Remove this patch and its patchedDependencies entry in pnpm-workspace.yaml once upstream ships a
fix. The entry names next@16.4.0, so changing next's version fails pnpm install until the patch is
re-made against the new version or removed.

